From bc1ec543c570c0e135b89f454ad4445b14859c28 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:22:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 16/16] mesa: grant a one-sample multisample renderbuffer one
 sample

RenderbufferStorageMultisample may grant more samples than the caller asked
for, but section 9.2.4 of the OpenGL 4.6 core specification bounds that at "the
next larger sample count supported by the implementation", and one sample is
supported everywhere. The frontend nevertheless started its search at two
samples whenever the driver had real multisampling, so a one-sample request and
a two-sample request were answered with the same count.

Two renderbuffers the caller gave different sample counts then carry the same
RENDERBUFFER_SAMPLES, section 9.4.2 requires the framebuffer to be reported
complete, and KHR-GL46.direct_state_access.framebuffers_check_status observes
that as a compatibility warning. Start the search at the requested count, so a
one-sample renderbuffer is one sample and a framebuffer that mixes it with a
two-sample renderbuffer is incomplete, as the caller described it.

Multisample textures keep the promotion. A texture is also sampled, and a
one-sample multisample texture has no Metal representation, so honouring the
request there costs the rendering it was meant to serve: with the same change
applied to st_texture_storage, 76 of the 112 KHR-GL46 `samples_1` cases in the
must-pass leaf fail on the Zink over Venus and KosmicKrisp stack, all of them
silent value mismatches in sample_variables and shader_multisample_interpolation.
Renderbuffers are only ever attachments and blit or read sources, so one sample
is representable for them everywhere.
---
 src/mesa/main/renderbuffer.c | 20 ++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/mesa/main/renderbuffer.c b/src/mesa/main/renderbuffer.c
index eb4701f..46f948e 100644
--- a/src/mesa/main/renderbuffer.c
+++ b/src/mesa/main/renderbuffer.c
@@ -168,16 +168,16 @@ renderbuffer_alloc_storage(struct gl_context * ctx,
     * Find the supported number of samples >= rb->NumSamples
     */
    if (rb->NumSamples > 0) {
-      unsigned start, start_storage;
-
-      if (ctx->Const.MaxSamples > 1 &&  rb->NumSamples == 1) {
-         /* don't try num_samples = 1 with drivers that support real msaa */
-         start = 2;
-         start_storage = 2;
-      } else {
-         start = rb->NumSamples;
-         start_storage = rb->NumStorageSamples;
-      }
+      /* Start the search at the requested count, one sample included.  The
+       * paragraph above allows a larger count only up to the next supported
+       * one, and one sample is supported everywhere, so a one-sample request
+       * is answered with one sample.  Answering it with two would make a
+       * one-sample renderbuffer indistinguishable from a two-sample one, and
+       * section 9.4.2 of the OpenGL 4.6 core specification would then have to
+       * call a framebuffer that mixes the two complete.
+       */
+      unsigned start = rb->NumSamples;
+      unsigned start_storage = rb->NumStorageSamples;
 
       if (ctx->Extensions.AMD_framebuffer_multisample_advanced) {
          if (rb->_BaseFormat == GL_DEPTH_COMPONENT ||
